---
import DesignSystemShell from "../../layouts/DesignSystemShell.astro";
import DsSection from "../../components/design-system/DsSection.astro";
import DsRule from "../../components/design-system/DsRule.astro";
import { DS_BASE, chapterHref, dsChapters } from "@lib/design-system/nav";
import { tokens } from "@lib/design-system/tokens";

const chapterCount = dsChapters.length;
const sectionCount = dsChapters.reduce((n, c) => n + c.sections.length, 0);
---

<DesignSystemShell slug="">
  <DsSection id="purpose" title="Purpose">
    <p>
      This site is the one design source of truth for everything Pro Exteriors puts in front of a person: the Command Center
      and every work surface in it, the agent and Slack surfaces, public web pages, printed collateral, and promotional
      items. It exists so that a designer, an engineer, or an AI agent can build a new surface — desktop or 100% mobile —
      without asking a single question that this site could have answered.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is written for the builder, but every rule in it is justified by the <strong>user</strong>: Lucinda working an
      invoice queue on a Friday, Roberto reading an order audit on a phone in a truck, Chandler scanning cash runway before
      a call, a homeowner reading a yard sign from the kerb. When a rule and a user's need disagree, the rule is wrong and
      the change is recorded in the <a href={`${DS_BASE}/decisions`}>decisions log</a>.
    </p>
    <div class="ds-grid-3">
      <div class="ds-card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Scope</p>
        <h3>{chapterCount} chapters · {sectionCount} sections</h3>
        <p>Tokens, logo, type, spacing, icons, every component, modes, motion, dashboards, decisions, mobile, accessibility, content, print, swag, marketing web, and the agent handbook.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ds-card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Live, not copied</p>
        <h3>{tokens.length} tokens read from production CSS</h3>
        <p>Every colour, radius, spacing and font value on this site is parsed from <code>global.css</code> at build time. Contrast ratios are measured, not asserted.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ds-card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Machine readable</p>
        <h3><a href={`${DS_BASE}/tokens.json`}>tokens.json</a></h3>
        <p>Agents read the token feed and the numbered rules (<code>C-04</code>, <code>T-02</code>…) and cite them in PRs. Rule ids never change once published.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </DsSection>

  <DsSection id="first-principles" title="First-principles method">
    <p>
      Every surface is designed by decomposing the company's work down to the facts that cannot be argued with, then
      rebuilding the screen from those facts up. The method is the same five questions each time, in order. A surface
      that skips a question is a guess dressed up as a design.
    </p>
    <div class="ds-table-wrap">
      <table class="ds-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>#</th><th>Question</th><th>What it forces</th><th>Example from the Command Center</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1</td><td><strong>What decision does the user have to make here, and what happens in the business if it is wrong?</strong></td><td>Names the one primary action and the cost of error. Sets how much evidence must be on screen.</td><td>Invoice Audit: "claim it, chase it, pay it, fix it" — four decisions, four KPI cards, one primary button per card. A wrong "pay it" moves real money, so the claim bar shows the negotiated price, the billed price and the variance side by side.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2</td><td><strong>What is the irreducible unit of work?</strong></td><td>Fixes row granularity, what a "record" is, and the key the user thinks in.</td><td>Friday WIP/AR: the job, keyed by the AccuLynx job name with the QBO Customer:Job suffix. Ten jobs is what a person can hold in a meeting, so a pane shows ten.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3</td><td><strong>Which facts are load-bearing and which are context?</strong></td><td>Load-bearing facts are always visible, tabular, and never colour-only. Context lives behind a disclosure.</td><td>Price audit lines: price per pricing UOM is load-bearing (compared in one unit, docs/46); vendor line description is context and truncates.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4</td><td><strong>What does the person already know, and what must the screen tell them?</strong></td><td>Removes chrome, labels the unfamiliar, states the cost of every control ("Show all 63 jobs ▾ (53 more)").</td><td>The scope control on Invoice Audit replaced three checkboxes: one question, "which slice am I looking at", answered once.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5</td><td><strong>What would make this wrong on a phone, in the dark, or for a screen reader?</strong></td><td>Mobile, dark mode and accessibility are constraints at the start, not a pass at the end.</td><td>Every audit surface ships light and dark from the same <code>cc.theme</code> preference; tables freeze the key column and scroll the rest.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="ds-callout">
      <b>Breaking the company down</b>
      <p>
        The Command Center is organised the way the business is organised — by department, then by the work each department
        does, then by the unit that work is done in (property → job → invoice → line). A new surface must be placeable in
        that tree before it is drawn. If it cannot be, the tree is missing a branch and that is a product decision first.
      </p>
    </div>
  </DsSection>

  <DsSection id="user-first" title="User over designer, user over client">
    <p>The order of precedence when tastes collide, from strongest to weakest claim on a design decision:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>The user's evidence.</strong> A recorded observation of a person failing, hesitating, or working around the screen. This outranks everything, including the client's stated preference, and is logged in the <a href={`${DS_BASE}/decisions#feedback`}>feedback log</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>The task's physics.</strong> What the data is, how much of it there is, how fast it changes, and what a wrong action costs.</li>
      <li><strong>This system's rules.</strong> Tokens, components and patterns that already exist. Reuse beats invention every time.</li>
      <li><strong>The client's brand.</strong> The inks, the logo, the tone — applied through the rules above, never in place of them.</li>
      <li><strong>The designer's preference.</strong> Last. A preference with no evidence behind it does not change a shipped surface.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      In practice this means: the brand's flag red is never used to decorate because the user needs it to mean "the one thing
      to do"; a beautiful chart is replaced by a table when the numbers are what the reader compares; a client request for a
      denser board is honoured only after the ten-row rule has been explained and the request repeated.
    </p>
  </DsSection>

  <DsSection id="sources" title="Sources of truth">
    <div class="ds-table-wrap">
      <table class="ds-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Artifact</th><th>Path</th><th>Owns</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Production tokens & component CSS</td><td><code>app/command-center/src/styles/global.css</code></td><td>Every value this site shows. The only place a token is defined.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Written specification</td><td><code>deployment/remote/dashboard/Design.md</code></td><td>Principles, five-color discipline, elevation, defensive styling. This site supersedes its rendered twin <code>design-system.html</code>.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Standard (pass/fail checks)</td><td><code>standards/design/v1.md</code></td><td>DSN-001 … DSN-020 and the long-list disclosure contract. QC owns it.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Conventions</td><td><code>CONVENTIONS.md</code> §11, §11a</td><td>Where the design system sits among the repo's other contracts.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Brand assets</td><td><code>config/brand/assets/</code></td><td>Official logo artwork and the stand-ins still awaiting official files.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Shared theme preference</td><td><code>app/command-center/src/scripts/theme-pref.ts</code></td><td>The one <code>cc.theme</code> key every surface reads.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>This site</td><td><code>app/command-center/src/pages/design-system/</code></td><td>The reading order, the rules with ids, the specimens, the decisions log.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Precedence when two disagree: the user's evidence, then <code>global.css</code> (what ships), then this site, then
      <code>Design.md</code>. Fix the source and regenerate; never patch a screenshot or a copy.
    </p>
  </DsSection>

  <DsSection id="reading" title="How to read this site">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Chapters</strong> are in the rail, grouped Foundations → Surfaces → Behaviour → Collateral → Governance. Read Foundations once; the rest is reference.</li>
      <li><strong>Rules</strong> are numbered cards (<code>C-01</code> is Color rule 1, <code>CMP-12</code> is Component rule 12). Cite the id in a PR or an issue. A rule's card names where it was recorded.</li>
      <li><strong>Specimens</strong> are real production markup in a labelled frame. Switch the theme in the top bar and every specimen re-renders in that mode.</li>
      <li><strong>Token chips</strong> copy the token name on click. Use the name, never the hex.</li>
      <li><strong>Search</strong> in the rail finds any chapter or section by name. <span class="ds-kbd">Enter</span> opens the first hit.</li>
      <li><strong>Agents</strong> start at the <a href={`${DS_BASE}/agents`}>handbook</a>: the build recipe, the ship checklist and the token feed.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="ds-grid-3">
      {dsChapters.filter((c) => c.slug).map((c) => (
        <a class="ds-card" href={chapterHref(c)} style="color:inherit;text-decoration:none">
          <p class="eyebrow">{c.group}</p>
          <h3>{c.title}</h3>
          <p style="color:var(--text-muted);font-size:13px">{c.summary}</p>
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>
  </DsSection>

  <DsSection id="rule-index" title="Rule index">
    <p>Rule prefixes, one per chapter. Every rule id on this site is unique and permanent.</p>
    <div class="ds-table-wrap">
      <table class="ds-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Prefix</th><th>Chapter</th><th>Prefix</th><th>Chapter</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>C-</code></td><td>Color</td><td><code>MO-</code></td><td>Motion</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>L-</code></td><td>Logo</td><td><code>D-</code></td><td>Dashboards & data</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>T-</code></td><td>Typography</td><td><code>UX-</code></td><td>UX decisions</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>S-</code></td><td>Spacing & layout</td><td><code>M-</code></td><td>Mobile</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>I-</code></td><td>Icons, imagery & alt text</td><td><code>A-</code></td><td>Accessibility</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>CMP-</code></td><td>Components</td><td><code>W-</code></td><td>Content & words</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>MD-</code></td><td>Light & dark modes</td><td><code>P-</code></td><td>Print</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>SW-</code></td><td>Swag</td><td><code>WEB-</code></td><td>Marketing web</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>G-</code></td><td>Governance (agent handbook)</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="ds-rules">
      <DsRule id="G-00" title="A surface that cannot cite the rules it follows is not done." source="this site, 2026-09-14">
        <p>Every PR that adds or changes a surface lists the rule ids it applied and any it deliberately broke, with the user evidence for the break. The ship checklist is in the <a href={`${DS_BASE}/agents#checklist`}>agent handbook</a>.</p>
      </DsRule>
    </div>
  </DsSection>
</DesignSystemShell>
